 "limitations": [
  "Simulation only. Users and items are synthetic.",
  "No claims about commercial systems. PULSE does not claim that any platform uses these rankers or produces these effects.",
  "No measurement of addiction or wellbeing. Attention is represented only by a position-based examination probability.",
  "Drift is an assumption. The drifting preference model encodes reinforcement by construction; EXP-002 measures how rankers interact with that assumption, not whether people drift.",
  "Narrow operationalisations. \"User control\" is responsiveness of slates to explicit dislikes; it is not human agency. \"Calibration\" follows a KL definition between latent preference and exposure.",
  "Simple rankers. No learned embedding model, no deep or RL ranker beyond LinUCB.",
  "Expected primary direction. The primary contrast is expected by construction; the informative parts are effect size, trade-offs and the sensitivity analysis.",
  "Licence. The repository is source-available under a non-commercial licence (D-001), not open source; reuse is restricted accordingly.",
  "MovieLens selection (round-2 data). The target like rate L* comes from ratings of movies that users chose to watch, so it is a selected-exposure like rate.",
  "Constructed preferences in EXP-004. Preference vectors are derived from like counts by add-one smoothing and division by the catalog share of each genre; genres are reduced to one primary topic per movie.",
  "One midpoint per tau. The fitted midpoint is held fixed across alpha_0, eta, pool skew and drift, so like rates differ by cell.",
  "Weak drift-form check (EXP-002). For a non-negative preference vector, additive-then-clip followed by normalisation equals the multiplicative rule with rate gamma/(1 + gamma) (0.0476 instead of 0.05) unless the clip at 1 binds.",
  "User control mixes responsiveness and slate volatility. The random arm's value (about -0.089) shows that slate topic shares change mechanically from step to step.",
  "Small seed-level variance. With 200 users per run the paired differences vary little across seeds (SD about 0.005 to 0.03), so very large d_z values and tiny p-values follow even for small effects (for example popularity minus random in H_exp, -0.0089).",
  "Calibration changed an exploratory pattern. Before the freeze, under the planning midpoint 0.5, the Zipf cells of the exploratory sweep showed similarity above random in H_exp (freeze record C4).",
  "One calibration per experiment family. The confirmatory results hold at the fitted midpoints; no confirmatory run varies the target like rate.",
  "Like-rate dependence (M4, exploratory evidence). Every confirmatory result holds at one calibrated like rate (0.577).",
  "Extent of source reading. Most related-work sources were read at the abstract page only; Steck 2018 only at its metadata record, so the miscalibration formula has not been checked against the paper; Harper and Konstan 2015 was not opened (citation from the GroupLens README)."
 ],
